        {
          "id": "SP_PROPHYSAON_DUBIUM_COCKERELL_1890",
          "name": "Prophysaon dubium Cockerell, 1890",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "Papillose taildropper",
          "lineage": "Prophysaon",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "<p style=\"margin: 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px Helvetica;\">External: Dorsal base color includes various shades of brown, reddish-brown, olive, and gray; mantle always mottled to some extent by areas of brown or gray pigment, which on some specimens merges to form broad, dark stripes; mantle and tail covered with numerous cone-shaped papillae; foot and to lesser extent the mantle, marked by light brown, orange, copper, and/or gold flecking; sole gray or cream.</p> <p style=\"margin: 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px Helvetica;\">Eggs: Translucent eggs are deposited in small clusters (6 to 14 eggs); approximately 1.3 x 1.2 mm diameter shortly after being laid and 2.1 x 1.5 mm 10 weeks later.</p>.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:2294058",
          "authorship": "Cockerell",
          "yearPublished": "1890",
